# C / C++ patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
C/C++ ownership, resource management, and their common anti-patterns.
Applies to both C and C++ files.

## RAII and ownership (C++)

- Every resource (heap memory, a file handle, a mutex lock, a socket) is
  owned by exactly one object whose destructor releases it — a raw `new`
  with a matching `delete` written by hand somewhere else in the function
  is a bug waiting for an early return or an exception to skip the
  `delete`. Wrap it in a smart pointer, `std::lock_guard`/`std::unique_lock`,
  or another RAII type instead.
- A class that manually manages a resource follows the Rule of
  Zero (own nothing directly — compose from types that already manage
  their own resources) or, when it must own a resource directly, the Rule
  of Five (copy ctor, copy assign, move ctor, move assign, destructor —
  define all five, or `= delete` the ones that should not exist).
- A function that hands back a resource does so by return value (RVO/move
  makes this free), not by an out-parameter raw pointer the caller must
  remember to release.
- Never return a pointer or reference to a local (stack) variable, or to a
  member of a temporary — the referent is destroyed when the function
  returns, and the caller is left with a dangling reference.

## Manual resource management (C)

- Pair every `malloc`/`fopen`/`pthread_mutex_lock`-style acquire with a
  `free`/`fclose`/`pthread_mutex_unlock` on every exit path from the
  function that acquired it, error paths included — a `goto cleanup;`
  pattern with a single exit-and-release block is the idiomatic way to
  keep this from being repeated (and missed) at every early `return`.
- A function that allocates and returns a pointer documents, in its
  header comment, who owns the result and how it must be released — an
  API with no stated ownership contract is a leak or a double-free
  waiting to happen at the call site.
- Never call `free` on a pointer twice, and set a pointer to `NULL`
  immediately after freeing it when it could otherwise be read or freed
  again on a later path — a double-free and a use-after-free are both
  more likely once a stale non-null value survives past its `free`.

## Containers and iterators

- An iterator, pointer, or reference into a `std::vector` (or similar
  contiguous container) is invalidated by any operation that can
  reallocate or shift the underlying storage (`push_back` past capacity,
  `insert`, `erase`) — do not hold one across such a call; re-fetch it, or
  use the iterator `erase`/`insert` return to keep iterating correctly.
- Prefer `std::vector`/`std::array`/`std::string` over a raw
  owning array or a hand-rolled dynamic buffer — the standard container
  already manages growth, bounds, and destruction correctly.

## Composition and interfaces

- Prefer composition over deep inheritance hierarchies; reach for
  polymorphism only when callers genuinely need to treat different
  concrete types uniformly through a shared interface, not as a default
  way to share code.
- A C++ base class meant to be used polymorphically through a base
  pointer/reference has a `virtual` (or `= default`d, explicitly
  `virtual`) destructor — a non-virtual destructor on a polymorphic base
  is undefined behavior when deleting through the base pointer.
- Keep a public C API's surface small and its ownership rules explicit in
  the header; do not expose an internal struct's full layout when an
  opaque handle plus accessor functions would let the implementation
  change without breaking callers.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A "god header" with unrelated free functions and macros accumulating
  under a generic name (`utils.h`, `common.h`) — split by what the code
  actually does.
- C-style casts (`(Type)x`) in C++ where a named cast
  (`static_cast`/`dynamic_cast`/`const_cast`/`reinterpret_cast`) would say
  which conversion is actually intended and let the compiler catch the
  wrong one.
- Output parameters used to fake multiple return values where
  `std::tuple`/`std::pair`/a small struct (or, in C, a returned struct)
  would be clearer and harder to call incorrectly.
